JVMTI (Java Virtual Machine Tool Interface) is an API that provides a way to instrument the JVM (Java Virtual Machine) and create classes on the fly. Here are some examples of JVMTI class transformations in Android:

**1. Implementing a simple interceptor**

In this example, we'll create a class that intercepts all method calls and prints the method name and arguments.
```java
// javaagent code ( MyClassTransformer.java )
import org.objectweb.asm.*; // add this dependency to yourpom.xml (maven)

public class MyClassTransformer implements ClassTransformer {
    @Override
    public byte[] transform(ClassLoader loader, String nameofClass, byte[] classfileBuffer) throws Exception {
        // Get the ClassFile object
        ClassFile cf = new ClassFile(classfileBuffer);

        // Iterate through the methods
        for (Method method : cf.methods) {
            // Implement the method interceptor
            method.transform(new MethodTransformer() {
                @Override
                public void transform(MethodVisitor mv) throws Exception {
                    mv.visitCode(); // jump to the method code
                    // print the method name and arguments
                    mv VisitLineNumber(1); // line numbers don't matter here
                    mv.visitFieldInsn(GETSTATIC, "java/lang/System", "out", "Ljava/io/PrinterStream;");
                    mv.visitFieldInsn(GETSTATIC, "java/lang/System", "out", "Ljava/io/PrinterStream;");
                    mv.visitMethodInsn(INVOKEVIRTUAL, "java/io/PrinterStream", "println", "(Ljava/lang/String;)V");
                    mv.visitCode(); // back to the start of the method
                }
            });
        }

        // Return the transformed class file
        return classfileBuffer;
    }
}
```
**2. Creating a dynamic proxy class**

In this example, we'll create a dynamic proxy class that proxy a method and print its arguments.
```java
// javaagent code ( MyProxyTransformer.java )
import org.objectweb.asm.*; // add this dependency to yourpom.xml (maven)

public class MyProxyTransformer implements ClassTransformer {
    @Override
    public byte[] transform(ClassLoader loader, String nameofClass, byte[] classfileBuffer) throws Exception {
        // Get the ClassFile object
        ClassFile cf = new ClassFile(classfileBuffer);

        // Create a new InnerClass object for the proxy class
        InnerClass proxyClass = new InnerClass();
        proxyClass.innerClassInfo.name = "ProxyClass";
        proxyClass.innerClassInfo.superName = nameofClass;

        // Generate the proxy class code (use ASMVisitor)
        MethodVisitor mv = new ProxyVisitor();
        cf.visit(this, mv);

        // Implement the method handler (use ASMMethodVisitor)
        mv.visitCode(); // jump to the method code
        mv.visitFieldInsn(GETSTATIC, "java/lang/reflect/Method", "getDeclaredMethods", "[Ljava/lang/reflect/Method;"); // get the method
        mv.visitVarInsn(ALOAD, 0); // load the this pointer
        mv.visitMethodInsn(INVOKEVIRTUAL, "java/lang/Object", "getDeclaredMethods", "()[Ljava/lang/reflect/Method;"); // call getDeclaredMethods()
        // Continue with the method handler implementation...
        mv.visitCode(); // back to the start of the method

        // Return the transformed class file
        return classfileBuffer;
    }
}

class ProxyVisitor extends ClassVisitor {
    @Override
    public MethodVisitor visitMethod(int access, String name, String desc, String signature, String[] exceptions) {
        return new ProxyMethodVisitor();
    }
}

class ProxyMethodVisitor extends MethodVisitor {
    @Override
    public void visitCode() {
        // Assemble the proxy method body...
    }
}
```
**3. Implementing a class loader**

In this example, we'll create a ClassLoader that loads classes from a specific directory.
```java
// javaagent code ( MyClassLoader.java )
import java.lang/instrument/ClassLoader;
import java.io.*;

public class MyClassLoader extends ClassLoader {
    public MyClassLoader(URL url) {
        super(url);
    }

    @Override
    protected Class<?> findClass(String name) throws ClassNotFoundException {
        // Load the class from the specific directory
        File file = new File("/path/to/classes/" + name + ".class");
        if (file.exists()) {
            return defineClass(name, file);
        } else {
            throw new ClassNotFoundException(name);
        }
    }
}
```
